  "query_preview": "..\n(This afternoon through Saturday)\nIssued at 1124 AM CDT SUN SEP 27 2026\n\nIf you take a quick glance at the KBMX radar this morning, you'd\nthink some light showers are moving into the area from the\nnorthwest. However, the 12Z KBMX sounding reveals an extremely\ndry layer just off the surface to about 700mb. It is pretty\nunlikely that any precipitation is making it through this much\ndrier air. As a result, we will just see some mid to upper level\nclouds streaming by through the day. Otherwise, with little\npattern change, we would expect to see another round of patchy\nriver fog again early Monday morning.\n\nThe upper level pattern begins to change early next week as a\nweak shortwave cuts into the Tennessee Valley on Monday and\nTuesday, followed by a stronger upper trough late in the work\nweek. However, limited moisture in place keeps dry conditions in\nplace through mid week. Low to medium chances for showers and\nstorms return to the forecast late in the week as the\naforementioned upper trough and accompanying cold front near the\nregion. There is a bit more uncertainty late in the week as there\nare both spatial and temporal differences between the latest\nmodel suite. We will keep a close eye on the model trends over\nthe coming days.\n\nWe kick off a warming trend as we head into the work week with\nhighs rebounding into the low 90s by Wednesday. Drier air in\nplace will help us maintain our cooler mornings with lows\ndropping down into the mid 50s to around 60 degrees through mid\nweek. As we head into the late stages of the work week, a moist\nsoutherly flow will bump out overnight lows up into the upper 60s\nto low 70s.\n\n95/Castillo",
